{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/635332-what-do-like-about-2nd-ed-over-v20","id":635332,"title":"What do like about 2nd ed over V20?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-08-21T19:40:47-07:00","id":635332,"title":"What do like about 2nd ed over V20?","content":"Hello everyone,<br /> <br /> Because I have limited time and limited resources I have to choose to either go with V20 or Vampire: The Requiem 2nd edition. This will likely mean I will purchase several books of only one edition. I have asked around for awhile but I still have not made a decision. It is like choosing to be with either your high school sweetheart or the girl/guy of your dreams. Tell me what do you like about 2nd edition as opposed to V20?<br /> <br /> Also, I have only been playing for about five years now with both cWoD and nWoD.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7397-olddog","name":"OldDog","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7397_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-21T21:31:07-07:00","id":635431,"title":"","content":"I'd recommend also asking this same question in the Masquerade forums. Opinions are undoubtedly going to be biased towards Requiem here and biased towards Masquerade there, so getting feedback from both groups will probably give you the most varied perspectives to help inform your decision.<br /> <br /> Personally, I'd go with Requiem 2e over V20 in a heartbeat. The rules are just much more streamlined and make for smoother gameplay. If it was a question between Requiem 2e and the upcoming Masquerade 4e, it might be a closer call, because M4 will presumably be made with an eye for more modern game design sensibilities, while preserving the nostalgia some folks have for Masquerade, and advancing the metaplot if you're into that. I would still go for Requiem because I don't have the same feelings of nostalgia for cWoD (as I learned nWoD first) and I'm not a fan of the metaplot. But if you do prefer the setting and style of Masquerade but prefer Requiem for the rules, waiting for Masquerade 4e is probably the way to go. They're shooting for a 2016 release too, so with luck it wouldn't even be that long of a wait.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-21T23:38:00-07:00","id":635480,"title":"","content":"Thanks for the reply. Wow...there is another edition of Masquerade coming out? Why exactly? How will this effect the current V20 rule set and the books already completed? <br /> <br /> Anyhow, I will also post this in the Masquerade section. However, I can always pickup a the PDF and check it out for myself. In fact...I think I will do it right now from the comfort of my local Internet Cafe.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7397-olddog","name":"OldDog","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7397_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-22T01:13:23-07:00","id":635503,"title":"","content":"I believe the reasoning is that V20 is more a comphendium of the three previous editions rather than really trying to develop the setting further. There's a bit of refinement but I think it's mostly as things were (I gather).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-22T01:27:21-07:00","id":635513,"title":"","content":"Comparing 1st editition requiem with masquerade, it was also a close call for me. Requiem has much more freedom and diversity in the possible stories and also much better rules, but masquerade on the other hand has its looming thousands-of-years-old threats and omens and end-of-the world feeling. Both is very compelling for me. But now, with blood and smoke (i really like the old name <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> ), the decision for me will always be towards requiem. The 2nd edition vampires are so much closer to my personal image of vampires, they &quot;feel&quot; so much more like vampires than either masquerade or requiem 1ed, that this is clearly my favourite game about vampires now.<br /> <br /> In my last requiem 2nd edition game, I really felt the dichotomy (if this is the correct word) between &quot;cool&quot; personal power on the on hand, and horror about what this power does to other people on the other hand, paired with the knowledge that even greater horrors than myself are out there. And this is how a vampire game should feel, in my opinion.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1125-gflash","name":"gflash","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1125_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"50"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-22T07:54:36-07:00","id":635807,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>OldDog</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/635332-what-do-like-about-2nd-ed-over-v20?p=635480#post635480\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Thanks for the reply. Wow...there is another edition of Masquerade coming out? Why exactly? How will this effect the current V20 rule set and the books already completed?</div> </div> </div> </div><a href=\"http://theonyxpath.com/vampire-the-masquerade-4th-edition-announced-at-gen-con/\" target=\"_blank\">Rich explained it better than I could</a>.<br /> The short version is, V20 was conceived as a compendium of everything that had come before, with some small tweaks here and there. It does that very well, but it doesn't work very well as a new edition. They're still going to finish V20's run, this is not a replacement for V20 - they fill very different roles. 4th edition, though, is going to update the rules and continue the metaplot. We know it will pick up in the same year the book releases (so hopefully 2016), which means post-Gehenna, but that it *won't* be post-apocalyptic.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-22T09:59:18-07:00","id":635961,"title":"","content":"While there is still overlap in content, it's not as pronounced as it was in the <b>Requiem</b> 1st era when it was the only <b>Vampire</b> game White Wolf was putting out. <b>Requiem</b> 2nd has a fairly different playstyle from R1, let alone <b>Masquerade</b>. Rule systems like Conditions and the various options to focus on Hunting give it a different feel even if you play a chronicle that would fit into either setting. <b>Masquerade</b> as a system mostly gets out of the way, <b>Requiem</b> 2nd should engage the players and ST throughout.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Craig Oxbrow<br /> <b>The Trinity Continuum</b> freelancer","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/221-craig-oxbrow","name":"Craig Oxbrow","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar221_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"608"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-22T10:26:42-07:00","id":635998,"title":"","content":"This feels like a trap, so I'm going to try and be careful.<br /> <br /> The rules are really smooth and, I feel, really get the feeling of playing a vampire across. And you're not burdened down by really specialized clans, plus you have more room to choose from with the opening of the covenants.<br /> <br /> That said, v20 is a compilation of Masquerade stuff, so it has almost all the options made available throughout the game's line, while Requiem's second edition is a core book and really sticks to getting the core vampire experience across to you, while having to wait for further books. I have no interest in Masquerade beyond a couple clans, so the decision is easy for me.<br /> <br /> All that said, in terms of resources v20 is more expensive and you could probably get both if you just got the pdfs.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-22T12:14:07-07:00","id":636158,"title":"","content":"It all depends too much on your personal style, interests and feelings. So its easier to help you if you tell us something of your expectations and feelings toward each game.<br /> <br /> But as a first warn, only very, very few people disagree about the rules on Requiem being far better. Even more so in 2nd edition. A lot of people still stick to Masquerade for reasons, or play both (as myself), but even among those most recognize that Masquerade rules are way too broken.<br /> <br /> The settings have too much distinctions to talk about. They're completely different games, even if similar at first. Since 1st edition, still too tied to Masquerade in some respects, Requiem wasn't based on the same premises and literary background (old-style Gothic Horror mixed with Punk-style social criticism for Masquerade). So they are vastly different experiences.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-24T16:09:58-07:00","id":638432,"title":"","content":"They're both great, and you can't really go wrong with either.<br /> <br /> Personally, I think the lore behind V:tM makes it the stronger line, but, like others have said, the rules are definitely smoother in V:tR. I also prefer the Camarilla-Anarch-Sabbat dichotemy to that of the Covenants, because it's feels like more of a social/class conflict than the covenant's Parliamentary political-style conflict, not that you can't have both with either.<br /> <br /> Go with whichever matters more to you.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":231,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/231-blitzburger","user":"Blitzburger","date":". 08-24-2015, 07:15 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/231-blitzburger","name":"Blitzburger","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar231_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"183"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-24T18:52:24-07:00","id":638626,"title":"","content":"A sound option is to take Requiem and the Translation Guide, them seek the lore you want from Masquerade to build your game. It is a way to have both options at a lower cost (and more solid rules, if not always perfect to simulate Masquerade).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-26T05:17:16-07:00","id":640078,"title":"","content":"I am in love with Masquerade setting and metaplot, but Requiem 2nd ed has much better rules. As already suggested by others, if you have the time, I would advise you to get Requiem 2nd ed and convert Masquerade material, with the help of the Translation Guide and some homebrew versions of Disciplines on the Requiem homebrew hub sticky thread here. It does work out pretty well, in practice.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2089-nerdokingoftrash","name":"nerdokingoftrash","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"23"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-26T06:28:15-07:00","id":640113,"title":"","content":"Both games are different enough that you're going to have completely different experiences with them. My preference is for <b><i>Requiem</i></b>, but I wouldn't play <b><i>Masquerade</i></b> for the same reasons, if that makes sense.<br /> <br /> To me, <b><i>Masquerade</i></b> is a good 90s game, where it feels like you're playing a bloody(er) version of <i>The Crow</i>. The cities are all run-down and full of corrupt cops and criminal conspiracies. There's a layer of stylized grime reminiscent (to me) of <b><i>Shadowrun</i></b>. Certainly you can play a sordid game of personal horror with <b><i>Masquerade</i></b>, but I'd greatly prefer <b><i>Requiem</i></b> for that (despite 2nd Edition <b><i>Masquerade</i></b>'s core book saying that it was a game about internal conflict and personal horror). I guess I've just come to believe that <i><b>Masquerade</b></i> is more about exploring a setting than about exploring your characters.<br /> <br /> Speaking of which, <b><i>Requiem</i></b> feels more like, say, <i>It Follows</i> or <i>Let the Right One In</i>. The style is dialed back, nothing is as obvious, vampires are <i>not</i> the scariest thing in the shadows. 2nd Edition especially is a game about monsters trying to be human, and the societies that they create to keep up the charade. And what happens when a boogeyman (the Strix) threatens to tear that apart from within. I've got a friend who says that the Kindred don't need a boogeyman, since they already had Antediluvians, but I like that the Strix <i>can</i> be fought, whereas if 3rd-generation vampires show up in your city in <b><i>Masquerade</i></b> you're all boned.<br /> <br /> Another subtle difference is that the punk elements of <i><b>Masquerade</b></i> lend themselves to class vs class, scrappy underdog stories. I don't buy that a Ventrue and a Gangrel would hang out there, because it doesn't fit the themes (though subversion is always available). <b><i>Requiem</i></b> doesn't have that built-in assumption of classism, but since the focus is on exploring the horror of the vampiric condition, and <i>every</i> Kindred experiences that, I could buy a Daeva pop-star hanging out with a Nosferatu middle-school janitor.<br /> <br /> Right now I'm biased toward <b><i>Requiem</i></b>, because I want subtler games about people, monsters, and their relationships to each other, but I see what <b><i>Masquerade</i></b> has on display and I can appreciate it for what it is.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/127887-doing-first-tongue-translations?p=484226#post484226\" target=\"_blank\">Nihhina kalekal-zidu kal masun, kal manudanadu. Nihhina kalekal-zidu nukal shaghu-desasudu \u2014 nihhina kalekal-zidu kal innu-desasudu udhkal samm</a>.&quot; Arthur Ashe<br /> She/her, contributing writer for <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/363404/Scion-Pride-2021\" target=\"_blank\">Scion: Pride 2021</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":15}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1923-bigdamnhero","name":"BigDamnHero","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1923_4.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1263"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-26T09:20:24-07:00","id":640323,"title":"","content":"Very well said, BDH.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-26T22:58:16-07:00","id":641160,"title":"","content":"Masquerade could actually be pretty good on the Personal Horror.<br /> <br /> On First Edition.<br /> <br /> Also when you actually assumed the PCs to not conform expectations. Then you could have Personal Horror.<br /> <br /> But in the end, yeah. Most of what <b>BigDamnHero</b> said.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-27T19:53:54-07:00","id":642391,"title":"","content":"I feel like its worth saying that Requiem is really good at doing action and exploration of a setting as well. If you are considering between Requiem 2e and V20, I would personally recommend Requeim 2e. The corebook is really evocative and well written and it has everything you need to run any style of vampire game, whether thats personal horror, politics, investigation, action or a mix of all of the above.<br /> <br /> Requiem's setting is less defined than Masquerade's. This gives a lot of freedom for storytellers and players to come up with cool settings, plots and characters. It possibly requires a little more work than using the more detailed Masquerade setting, I guess but not much more. There are several good supplements you can use for inspiration and I love the freedom the broad archtypes allows in play. If you have a character concept, chances are it would fit any of the five archtypal clans in someway, while the more specific clans of Masquerade I find a bit limiting. The same can go for political structures in a city and the archytypal groups.<br /> <br /> In my opinion the main problem with Requiem is that its less popular (and known) than Masquerade. I've played both and prefer Requiem by quite a bit, although I must admit I have played a lot more Requiem than Masquerade.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1161-gravitas-man","name":"Gravitas Man","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1161_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"95"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}